// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 3): animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"moose",t:"Moose",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🫎",w:"Moose",r:["deer","reindeer","wolf","conservation","swedencountry"],
p:[["The biggest deer","The moose is the largest member of the deer family."],["Northern forests","Moose live in the cold forests of North America, Europe and Asia."],["Called elk in Europe","In Britain and Europe, the same animal is usually called an elk."]],
l:"The moose is the largest living member of the deer family, found in the boreal and mixed forests of northern North America, Scandinavia, the Baltic states, Russia and northern China. A large bull can stand more than 2 meters tall at the shoulder and weigh up to about 700 kilograms. Males grow huge, flat, palm-shaped antlers that can span about 1.8 meters, which they shed each winter and regrow in spring. Moose have long legs for wading through snow and deep water, a drooping nose and a flap of skin called a bell hanging from the throat. They eat leaves, twigs, bark and water plants, which they reach by diving several meters underwater. Moose usually live alone, except for mothers with calves. In British English the animal is called an elk, while in North America ‘elk’ means a different deer, the wapiti. Moose are important to Indigenous peoples and hunters, but collisions with cars are dangerous, and warming winters have increased deadly tick infestations.",
s:[["Body","A moose’s long legs let it step over fallen logs and move through snow up to about a meter deep. Its hollow hairs insulate it from the cold, but moose overheat easily above about 14°C in winter and 20°C in summer, so they rest in shade or water on warm days."],["Antlers","Only males grow antlers. They grow at up to about 2.5 centimeters a day in summer, covered in soft velvet, and are used in autumn to impress females and fight rivals. A large set can weigh about 30 kilograms."],["Moose and water","Moose are strong swimmers and can cross wide lakes and rivers. They feed on water lilies and other aquatic plants rich in sodium and can stay underwater for about a minute, closing their nostrils."],["Threats","Winter ticks, which survive better in milder winters, can number tens of thousands on a single moose, causing many calves to die in parts of New England. Brain worms carried by white-tailed deer, wolves and bears also affect moose populations."]],
k:[["Family","Deer"],["Height","Up to about 2.1 m at the shoulder"],["Weight","Up to about 700 kg"],["Antler span","Up to about 1.8 m"],["European name","Elk"]],
d:[["1600s","English settlers adopt the Algonquian word ‘moose’"],["1900s","Moose reintroduced to parts of the northeastern United States"],["1997","Swedish ‘moose test’ tips over a new car"],["2010s","Winter ticks cause heavy moose losses in New England"]],
s2:[["The moose test","Sweden tests how cars handle sudden swerves to avoid a moose on the road, called the älgtest. In 1997, a new small car rolled over during the test, forcing its maker to add stability systems. Moose collisions are a major road danger in Scandinavia and Canada."],["Moose and wolves","On Isle Royale in Lake Superior, scientists have studied moose and wolves together since 1958, one of the longest predator–prey studies in the world. When wolf numbers fell, moose multiplied and overgrazed the forest, until new wolves were brought in from 2018."]],
sp:["The word moose","The English word ‘moose’ comes from an Algonquian language word, often translated as ‘twig eater’ or ‘he strips off bark’, referring to how moose feed in winter."],
g:[["Boreal forest","The cold northern forest of conifers"],["Antler","A bony growth shed and regrown each year"],["Velvet","Soft skin covering growing antlers"],["Bell","A flap of skin under a moose’s throat"]],
f:["The moose is the largest deer.","Moose can dive underwater to feed.","In Britain the moose is called an elk."],
fun:"A moose’s antlers are among the fastest-growing tissues of any animal, adding up to about 2.5 centimeters a day."});

ADDART({id:"jaguars",t:"Jaguars",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐆",w:"Jaguar",r:["leopards","tiger","amazon","maya","conservation"],
p:[["Big cat of the Americas","The jaguar is the largest cat in the Americas."],["Powerful bite","Jaguars kill prey by biting through skulls and turtle shells."],["Loves water","Unlike most cats, jaguars swim well and hunt in rivers."]],
l:"The jaguar is a large wild cat native to the Americas, from the southwestern United States and Mexico through Central America to northern Argentina, with its stronghold in the Amazon rainforest and the Pantanal wetlands of Brazil. It is the third-largest cat in the world, after the tiger and lion, and the largest in the Americas, weighing up to about 150 kilograms. Jaguars have a golden coat covered in rosettes, rings with spots inside, which distinguish them from leopards; some are almost black. They are solitary, ambush predators with an exceptionally strong bite for their size, often killing prey by biting directly through the skull, and can crack turtle shells. Jaguars eat more than 80 kinds of prey, including capybaras, deer, peccaries, caimans and fish, and are excellent swimmers. They were sacred to the Olmec, Maya and Aztec peoples. Jaguars are listed as near threatened because of deforestation, conflict with ranchers and illegal trade in their body parts; scientists estimated about 173,000 jaguars remained in 2016.",
s:[["Jaguar or leopard?","Jaguars and leopards look similar, but jaguars are stockier, with bigger heads and shorter tails, and their rosettes usually have spots in the middle. Leopards live in Africa and Asia, so a spotted big cat in the wild in the Americas is a jaguar."],["Hunting","Jaguars hunt mostly at dawn and dusk, stalking and ambushing prey. In the Pantanal they regularly catch caimans in rivers, diving after them. Their name may come from a Tupi–Guarani word, yaguara, often said to mean ‘one who kills with one leap’."],["Jaguars and people","The Olmec carved were-jaguar figures, Maya kings wore jaguar skins and took names such as Jaguar Paw, and Aztec elite warriors were called jaguar warriors. The jaguar remains a national symbol in Brazil, Mexico and other countries."],["Conservation","Jaguars have lost about half of their historic range. Wildlife corridors, such as the Jaguar Corridor Initiative, aim to connect populations from Mexico to Argentina. A few jaguars have been seen in Arizona since the 1990s, crossing from Mexico."]],
k:[["Range","Americas, from Mexico to Argentina"],["Rank","Third-largest cat"],["Weight","Up to about 150 kg"],["Markings","Rosettes with central spots"],["Status","Near threatened"]],
d:[["c. 1200 BC","Olmec culture reveres the jaguar"],["1758","Linnaeus names the jaguar Felis onca"],["1960s–1970s","Hundreds of thousands killed for the fur trade"],["1975","International trade in jaguar skins banned (CITES)"],["2016","Scientists estimate about 173,000 jaguars"]],
s2:[["The fur trade","In the 1960s and early 1970s, tens of thousands of jaguar skins were exported each year for fashion. A ban on international trade under CITES in 1975 helped stop the slaughter, but jaguar teeth and body parts are still trafficked."],["Black jaguars","Black jaguars have a gene that produces extra dark pigment, but their rosettes are still faintly visible. They are sometimes called black panthers, a name also used for black leopards."]],
sp:["The Pantanal","The Pantanal, the world’s largest tropical wetland, has one of the highest densities of jaguars. Jaguar-watching tourism there has become worth more to local communities than cattle ranching in some areas."],
g:[["Rosette","A rose-shaped spot pattern"],["Ambush predator","An animal that hides and attacks suddenly"],["Wildlife corridor","Habitat that connects separated populations"],["CITES","An international treaty controlling wildlife trade"]],
f:["The jaguar is the largest cat in the Americas.","Jaguars can bite through turtle shells.","Jaguars are strong swimmers."],
fun:"A jaguar’s bite is strong enough that it often kills prey with a single bite to the back of the skull, something few other big cats do."});

ADDART({id:"squirrels",t:"Squirrels",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐿️",w:"Squirrel",r:["rodents","trees","oaks","invasiveplants","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Busy rodents","Squirrels are a family of rodents that includes tree squirrels, chipmunks and marmots."],["Planting forests","Forgotten nuts buried by squirrels grow into new trees."],["Gliders","Flying squirrels glide between trees using skin flaps."]],
l:"Squirrels are a family of about 280 species of rodents found on every continent except Australia and Antarctica. The family includes tree squirrels with bushy tails, ground squirrels, chipmunks, prairie dogs and marmots, and flying squirrels that glide between trees. Squirrels range in size from the African pygmy squirrel, about 10 centimeters long, to the Alpine marmot and Indian giant squirrel, which can be about a meter long including the tail. Most eat nuts, seeds, fruit and fungi, and some eat insects, eggs and small animals. Many squirrels store food for winter: some hide thousands of nuts in separate places and find them later using memory and smell, but forgotten nuts often sprout, helping forests spread. Squirrels have excellent vision and agility and can rotate their back feet to climb headfirst down trees. In Britain, introduced North American gray squirrels have largely replaced native red squirrels.",
s:[["Caching food","Eastern gray squirrels bury nuts one at a time in scattered places, called scatter-hoarding, and can remember the locations of many of them. Studies suggest that squirrels may organize their caches by type of nut. Red squirrels in North America instead store cones in large central piles called middens."],["Flying squirrels","Flying squirrels have a furry membrane, the patagium, stretching between their front and back legs. By spreading it, they can glide up to about 90 meters between trees, steering with their tails. In 2019, scientists found that some flying squirrels glow pink under ultraviolet light."],["Red versus gray","Gray squirrels were introduced to Britain from North America from 1876. They are larger, better at digesting acorns and carry a squirrelpox virus that kills red squirrels. Red squirrels now survive mainly in Scotland, islands and protected forests, helped by the return of pine martens, which prey more on grays."],["Ground squirrels","Prairie dogs live in huge underground ‘towns’ with complex warning calls. Marmots hibernate for up to eight months. The groundhog, a marmot, is the focus of Groundhog Day in the United States, celebrated each 2 February since 1887 in Punxsutawney, Pennsylvania."]],
k:[["Family","Sciuridae"],["Species","About 280"],["Absent from","Australia and Antarctica"],["Smallest","African pygmy squirrel"],["Gliding distance","Up to about 90 m"]],
d:[["1876","Gray squirrels introduced to Britain"],["1887","First Groundhog Day in Punxsutawney"],["1930s","Gray squirrels declared pests in Britain"],["2019","Flying squirrels found to glow pink under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["Squirrel-proof feeders","Squirrels are famous for raiding bird feeders, solving obstacles and leaping long distances. In a 2021 study, squirrels performed parkour-like leaps, adjusting their jumps based on how bendy branches were."],["Squirrels and trees","By burying acorns and other seeds far from the parent tree, squirrels help oaks, walnuts and pines spread. Scientists estimate that a single gray squirrel may bury thousands of nuts each autumn."]],
sp:["Indian giant squirrel","The Indian giant squirrel, or Malabar giant squirrel, has a brilliantly colored coat of maroon, orange, cream and black and can leap about 6 meters between trees in the forests of India."],
g:[["Rodent","A mammal with constantly growing front teeth"],["Cache","A hidden store of food"],["Patagium","The skin membrane used by gliding animals"],["Hibernate","To spend winter in a deep, sleep-like state"]],
f:["Forgotten squirrel nuts can grow into trees.","Flying squirrels glide, not fly.","Squirrels can climb down trees headfirst."],
fun:"A squirrel’s front teeth never stop growing, about 15 centimeters a year, so it must keep gnawing to wear them down."});

ADDART({id:"tapirs",t:"Tapirs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐗",w:"Tapir",r:["rhino","horses","amazon","conservation","malaysiacountry"],
p:[["Living fossils","Tapirs have changed little in tens of millions of years."],["A short trunk","Their flexible snout works like a small trunk."],["Gardeners of the forest","Tapirs spread seeds across forests."]],
l:"Tapirs are large, plant-eating mammals with stout bodies, short legs and a flexible, trunk-like snout. They are related to horses and rhinoceroses. There are four widely recognized living species: three in Central and South America, the Brazilian or lowland tapir, the mountain tapir and Baird’s tapir, and one in Southeast Asia, the black-and-white Malayan tapir. Tapirs live in forests, grasslands and mountain areas near water and are good swimmers, often escaping predators by diving into rivers. They eat leaves, fruit and water plants and help forests by spreading seeds in their droppings over long distances. Tapirs have existed in similar form for about 20 million years or more. Baby tapirs are born with striped and spotted coats, like watermelons, that provide camouflage. All four species are threatened by habitat loss and hunting, with the mountain, Baird’s and Malayan tapirs listed as endangered.",
s:[["The snout","A tapir’s snout, or proboscis, is formed from the nose and upper lip. It can stretch, grab leaves and fruit, and act as a snorkel when swimming. Tapirs have an excellent sense of smell but poor eyesight."],["Seed dispersers","Lowland tapirs swallow large fruits and travel long distances, dropping seeds far from parent trees. Scientists call them ‘gardeners of the forest’, and studies in Brazil found that tapirs help regrow damaged rainforest."],["The Malayan tapir","The Malayan tapir’s black front and back and white middle break up its outline in moonlit forests, making it hard for tigers to see. It lives in Myanmar, Thailand, Malaysia and Sumatra and is endangered by deforestation for palm oil plantations."],["Ancient animals","Tapir-like animals once lived across North America, Europe and Asia. Fossils show their basic body plan has changed little for millions of years, earning them the label ‘living fossils’."]],
k:[["Relatives","Horses and rhinoceroses"],["Living species","Four widely recognized"],["Range","Central and South America, Southeast Asia"],["Special feature","Trunk-like snout"],["Baby coat","Stripes and spots"]],
d:[["c. 20 million years ago","Tapirs of modern form appear"],["1819","Malayan tapir described by Western scientists"],["1865","Baird’s tapir named"],["1981","Baird’s tapir becomes the national animal of Belize"],["2008","International Tapir Day first held (27 April)"]],
s2:[["Mountain tapir","The mountain tapir of the Andes has thick, woolly fur to survive cold cloud forests and páramo grasslands at up to about 4,500 meters. Fewer than 2,500 adults are thought to remain."],["The dream eater","In Japanese folklore, the baku is a creature that eats nightmares. The modern Japanese word for tapir is also baku, because the mythical creature was often drawn with a tapir-like trunk."]],
sp:["Watermelon babies","Tapir calves are born with reddish-brown coats covered in white stripes and spots, which camouflage them in dappled forest light. The pattern fades by about six months of age."],
g:[["Proboscis","A long, flexible snout"],["Seed dispersal","Spreading seeds to new places"],["Living fossil","A species little changed over millions of years"],["Páramo","High, cold grasslands of the Andes"]],
f:["Tapirs are related to horses and rhinos.","Baby tapirs look like watermelons.","Tapirs use their snouts as snorkels."],
fun:"The Malayan tapir’s black-and-white pattern makes it look like a black animal wearing a white blanket."});

ADDART({id:"iguanas",t:"Iguanas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Iguana",r:["lizard","galapagos","invasiveplants","darwin","pets"],
p:[["Big plant-eating lizards","Iguanas are large lizards of the Americas and Caribbean."],["Sea-going iguanas","Galápagos marine iguanas are the only lizards that feed in the sea."],["A third eye","Iguanas have a light-sensing ‘third eye’ on top of the head."]],
l:"Iguanas are large lizards of tropical Central and South America and the Caribbean. The best known is the green iguana, which can grow to about 1.5 to 2 meters long including its tail and lives in trees near water. Iguanas have a row of spines down the back, a flap of skin called a dewlap under the throat, and sharp claws for climbing. Adults eat mainly leaves, flowers and fruit. Like many lizards, they have a pale scale on top of the head called the parietal eye, which senses light and shadow and may help them detect birds of prey. The Galápagos Islands are home to marine iguanas, the only lizards that forage in the sea, diving to eat algae, and to land iguanas. Rock iguanas of the Caribbean are among the most endangered lizards in the world. Green iguanas are popular pets and have become invasive in Florida, Puerto Rico and elsewhere, where cold snaps sometimes leave stunned iguanas falling from trees.",
s:[["Green iguanas","Green iguanas bask in the sun to warm up and can drop from branches into water from heights of 12 meters or more without injury. They are excellent swimmers and can stay underwater for nearly half an hour. Males defend territories by bobbing their heads and extending their dewlaps."],["Marine iguanas","Charles Darwin called marine iguanas ‘imps of darkness’. They dive in cold Galápagos waters to graze algae from rocks, then warm up on black lava. They sneeze out excess salt through glands near their nostrils, and during El Niño food shortages, they can shrink in body length to survive."],["Rock iguanas","Caribbean rock iguanas, such as the Grand Cayman blue iguana, were driven near extinction by cats, dogs, habitat loss and road traffic. Captive breeding raised the blue iguana from about 25 wild animals in 2002 to more than 1,000."],["Falling iguanas","Iguanas are cold-blooded and become sluggish when temperatures drop below about 10°C. During cold snaps in South Florida, such as in January 2020 and February 2025, weather forecasters warned residents of iguanas falling from trees."]],
k:[["Best-known species","Green iguana"],["Length","Up to about 2 m"],["Diet","Mainly leaves, flowers and fruit"],["Sea-feeding species","Galápagos marine iguana"],["Invasive in","Florida, Puerto Rico and elsewhere"]],
d:[["1835","Darwin studies marine iguanas in the Galápagos"],["1960s","Green iguanas established in Florida"],["2002","Only about 25 wild blue iguanas remain"],["2012","Blue iguana downgraded from critically endangered"],["2020","Cold snap brings ‘falling iguana’ warnings in Florida"]],
s2:[["Iguanas as food","In parts of Central America, iguana meat and eggs are traditionally eaten and nicknamed ‘chicken of the trees’. Iguana farming has been promoted to reduce hunting of wild animals."],["Pink iguana","A pink land iguana was discovered on Wolf Volcano in the Galápagos in 1986 and recognized as a separate species in 2009. Only a few hundred survive, all on that one volcano."]],
sp:["The third eye","The parietal eye has a simple lens and retina but cannot form images. It detects changes in light and may help iguanas sense predators from above and regulate daily and seasonal rhythms."],
g:[["Dewlap","A flap of skin under the throat"],["Parietal eye","A light-sensing organ on top of the head"],["Cold-blooded","Relying on outside heat for body temperature"],["Bask","To lie in the sun to warm up"]],
f:["Marine iguanas are the only sea-feeding lizards.","Iguanas have a light-sensing third eye.","Cold iguanas can fall from trees."],
fun:"Marine iguanas can shrink their skeletons by up to about 20 percent when food is scarce, then grow back when times improve."});

ADDART({id:"pelicans",t:"Pelicans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Pelican",r:["birds","puffins","fish","pollution","albatross"],
p:[["Built-in fishing nets","Pelicans scoop fish with huge stretchy throat pouches."],["Diving and herding","Brown pelicans plunge-dive, while white pelicans fish in teams."],["A comeback","Brown pelicans recovered after the pesticide DDT was banned."]],
l:"Pelicans are large water birds with long bills and enormous, stretchy throat pouches that they use to catch fish. There are eight species, living on every continent except Antarctica, along coasts, lakes and rivers. The Dalmatian pelican, one of the largest flying birds, can have a wingspan of more than 3 meters. A pelican’s pouch can hold about 11 liters of water; after scooping up fish, the bird tilts its head to drain the water before swallowing. The brown and Peruvian pelicans plunge-dive from the air into the sea, while other species, such as the American white pelican, swim in groups to herd fish into shallow water. Pelicans are strong fliers that soar on rising air and often fly in lines or V-shaped formations. In the mid-20th century, the pesticide DDT made pelican eggshells thin and fragile, and brown pelicans nearly vanished from parts of the United States; after DDT was banned in 1972, they recovered and were removed from the US endangered species list in 2009.",
s:[["The pouch","The pouch is made of stretchy skin hanging from the lower bill. Besides catching fish, pelicans flutter it to cool down on hot days. Pelicans do not store fish in their pouches; they swallow them quickly or carry them in the throat to their chicks."],["Fishing styles","Brown pelicans dive from heights of up to about 20 meters, folding their wings back just before hitting the water. Air sacs under their skin cushion the impact and help them bob back up. Great white pelicans in Africa fish cooperatively, forming a semicircle and beating their wings to drive fish toward the shore."],["Breeding","Pelicans nest in colonies on islands, in trees or on the ground. Parents feed chicks partly digested fish, and older chicks reach deep into their parents’ pouches and throats. Some colonies contain thousands of birds."],["Threats","Besides past pesticide poisoning, pelicans are harmed by oil spills, fishing lines and hooks, and disturbance of nesting sites. The 2010 Deepwater Horizon oil spill in the Gulf of Mexico coated many brown pelicans in oil. In 2022, bird flu killed many Dalmatian pelicans in Greece."]],
k:[["Species","Eight"],["Pouch capacity","About 11 liters"],["Largest","Dalmatian pelican, wingspan over 3 m"],["Diving species","Brown and Peruvian pelicans"],["Recovery","Brown pelican delisted in the US, 2009"]],
d:[["1903","Pelican Island becomes the first US national wildlife refuge"],["1970","Brown pelican listed as endangered in the US"],["1972","DDT banned in the United States"],["2009","Brown pelican removed from the US endangered list"],["2010","Deepwater Horizon spill harms pelicans"],["2022","Bird flu kills many Dalmatian pelicans in Greece"]],
s2:[["Pelican Island","In 1903, US President Theodore Roosevelt made Pelican Island in Florida the country’s first national wildlife refuge, to protect pelicans and other birds from hunters seeking feathers for fashionable hats."],["The pelican in Christianity","A medieval legend said that a mother pelican fed her chicks with her own blood by piercing her breast. The ‘pelican in her piety’ became a Christian symbol of sacrifice and appears in church art and heraldry."]],
sp:["Prehistoric pelicans","A 30-million-year-old fossil from France shows that pelicans with almost the same bill and pouch existed long ago, suggesting the design has changed little since then."],
g:[["Throat pouch","A stretchy skin bag under the bill"],["Plunge-dive","Diving into water from flight"],["DDT","A pesticide that thinned bird eggshells"],["Colony","A group of birds nesting together"]],
f:["A pelican’s pouch holds about 11 liters of water.","Brown pelicans recovered after DDT was banned.","Some pelicans fish in cooperative teams."],
fun:"Pelicans sometimes try to swallow surprising things, and one in a London park was filmed swallowing a pigeon whole, though it later let it go."});

ADDART({id:"toucans",t:"Toucans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Toucan",r:["birds","amazon","parrot","hornbill","seeds"],
p:[["Giant colorful bills","Toucans are tropical birds with huge, bright bills."],["Light but large","The bill is light because it is mostly hollow inside."],["Natural radiators","Toucans use their bills to release heat."]],
l:"Toucans are birds of the rainforests and woodlands of Central and South America, famous for their enormous, brightly colored bills. There are about 40 species, including smaller toucanets and aracaris. The largest, the toco toucan, has an orange bill about 20 centimeters long, about a third of its total length. Despite its size, a toucan’s bill is light: it is made of keratin, the material of fingernails, over a honeycomb of bone and air spaces. Toucans use their bills to reach and peel fruit at the ends of branches, and also eat insects, eggs and small animals. In 2009, scientists found that toucans control blood flow to their bills to release body heat, using the bill like a radiator. Toucans nest in tree holes, often old woodpecker holes, and sleep with their bills tucked over their backs. They help forests by spreading seeds. Some species are threatened by deforestation and capture for the pet trade.",
s:[["The bill","Scientists have long debated why toucan bills are so large. The bill may help attract mates, scare other birds away from fruit trees and reach food. Its serrated edges help grip fruit, and a long, narrow tongue helps move food to the throat."],["Keeping cool","Using infrared cameras, researchers in Brazil showed that toco toucans can quickly increase blood flow to the bill to shed heat when warm and reduce it to save heat when they sleep. The bill can release up to about 60 percent of body heat."],["Social life","Toucans live in pairs or small flocks and call loudly with croaks and yelps. They toss fruit to each other and fence playfully with their bills, behavior that may strengthen social bonds."],["Toucans and forests","Because toucans swallow fruits whole and later regurgitate or pass the seeds, they carry seeds of many trees across the forest. When large toucans disappear, trees with large seeds may struggle to spread."]],
k:[["Range","Central and South America"],["Species","About 40"],["Largest","Toco toucan"],["Bill made of","Keratin over bony honeycomb"],["Bill use","Feeding, display and cooling"]],
d:[["1550s","Toucans described in Europe after Spanish and Portuguese voyages"],["1758","Linnaeus names the toco toucan"],["2009","Study shows toucans use bills to shed heat"],["2015","Toucan with broken bill gets a 3D-printed replacement in Costa Rica"]],
s2:[["Grecia’s new beak","In 2015, a chestnut-mandibled toucan in Costa Rica named Grecia lost half of its upper bill after being attacked. Volunteers designed a 3D-printed prosthetic bill, which helped the bird eat and became famous worldwide."],["Toucans and hornbills","Hornbills of Africa and Asia look similar to toucans, with large bills, but they are not closely related. Their resemblance is an example of convergent evolution, in which unrelated animals evolve similar features."]],
sp:["Name","The word toucan comes from the Tupi language of Brazil, possibly imitating the bird’s call."],
g:[["Keratin","The tough protein in hair, nails and bills"],["Regurgitate","To bring swallowed food back up"],["Convergent evolution","Unrelated species evolving similar features"],["Aracari","A smaller kind of toucan"]],
f:["A toucan’s bill is mostly hollow.","Toucans use their bills to cool down.","Toucans and hornbills are not close relatives."],
fun:"When sleeping, a toucan turns its head and lays its bill along its back, then folds its tail forward to cover it, becoming a feathery ball."});

ADDART({id:"tardigrades",t:"Tardigrades",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐻",w:"Tardigrade",r:["microscopes","extremeheat","astrobio","ferns","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Water bears","Tardigrades are tiny eight-legged animals nicknamed water bears."],["Almost indestructible","They survive drying out, freezing, radiation and even the vacuum of space."],["Found everywhere","Tardigrades live from mountaintops to the deep sea."]],
l:"Tardigrades, also called water bears or moss piglets, are microscopic animals, usually less than a millimeter long, with plump bodies and eight stubby legs ending in claws. About 1,300 species have been described, living in moss, lichen, soil, leaf litter, fresh water and the ocean, on every continent including Antarctica. They eat plant cells, algae, bacteria and sometimes other tiny animals, piercing them with needle-like mouthparts. Tardigrades are famous for surviving extreme conditions by entering a state called cryptobiosis: they dry out, curl into a barrel shape called a tun and almost stop their metabolism. In this state they have survived temperatures close to absolute zero and up to about 150°C for a short time, pressures greater than in the deepest ocean, high radiation and, in a 2007 experiment, exposure to the vacuum of space. They revive when water returns. Scientists study tardigrade proteins, such as Dsup, for ways to protect cells and preserve medicines.",
s:[["Discovery","German pastor Johann August Ephraim Goeze first described tardigrades in 1773, calling them ‘little water bears’. Three years later, Italian biologist Lazzaro Spallanzani named them tardigrada, meaning ‘slow steppers’."],["Cryptobiosis","As a tardigrade dries out, it replaces water in its cells with protective sugars and special proteins that form glass-like structures, protecting its molecules. It can stay in this state for years, perhaps decades, and revive within hours when wet."],["Tardigrades in space","In 2007, the European FOTON-M3 mission exposed dried tardigrades to space for 10 days. Many survived the vacuum, and some survived the Sun’s ultraviolet radiation too. In 2019, the Israeli Beresheet lander crashed on the Moon carrying dehydrated tardigrades, though scientists believe they could not survive there long."],["Dsup","In 2016, Japanese scientists found a protein in one tardigrade species, which they named Dsup, for ‘damage suppressor’, that protects DNA from radiation. Human cells given the protein in the laboratory suffered less damage from X-rays."]],
k:[["Nicknames","Water bears, moss piglets"],["Size","Usually under 1 mm"],["Species","About 1,300"],["Survival state","Cryptobiosis (tun)"],["Space test","FOTON-M3, 2007"]],
d:[["1773","Goeze describes ‘little water bears’"],["1776","Spallanzani names them tardigrades"],["2007","Tardigrades survive exposure to space"],["2016","Dsup protein discovered"],["2019","Tardigrades crash on the Moon aboard Beresheet"]],
s2:[["Not quite indestructible","Tardigrades are tough only when dried out. Active tardigrades are much more fragile, and a 2020 study found that they could not survive long in water warmer than about 37°C. Climate change could therefore affect them."],["How to find one","Tardigrades are easy to find: soak a clump of moss in water overnight, then examine drops of the water under a simple microscope at about 30 to 40 times magnification. They often look like slow, plump bears waddling through the water."]],
sp:["Eight legs","Tardigrades have four pairs of legs, each ending in claws or sticky discs. They walk with a gait similar to insects, despite their very different body plan and tiny brains."],
g:[["Microscopic","Too small to see without a microscope"],["Cryptobiosis","A state in which life processes almost stop"],["Tun","The curled, dried state of a tardigrade"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes of life"]],
f:["Tardigrades have survived the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades have eight legs.","Dried tardigrades can revive when wet."],
fun:"Tardigrades crash-landed on the Moon in 2019, making them some of the first animals to reach its surface, though they almost certainly did not survive."});

ADDART({id:"chickens",t:"Chickens",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐔",w:"Chicken",r:["birds","domestication","agri","dino","pets"],
p:[["The most common bird","There are more chickens than any other bird on Earth."],["From the jungle","Chickens descend from the red junglefowl of Southeast Asia."],["Eggs and meat","Chickens are the world’s main source of eggs and a leading meat."]],
l:"The chicken is a domesticated bird kept for eggs and meat, and the most numerous bird in the world, with more than 25 billion alive at any time. Chickens descend mainly from the red junglefowl of the forests of Southeast Asia. A 2022 study suggested they were domesticated only about 3,500 years ago, probably in Thailand or nearby, after rice and millet farming attracted junglefowl to fields; at first they may have been kept for cockfighting and ceremonies rather than food. Chickens spread across Asia, Africa, Europe and the Pacific, and later around the world. Today hundreds of breeds exist, from tiny bantams to large meat breeds. Modern egg-laying hens can lay about 300 eggs a year, while broiler chickens are bred to reach market weight in about six weeks. Most are raised in large industrial farms, raising concerns about animal welfare, antibiotics and disease, especially bird flu, which has killed or forced the culling of hundreds of millions of poultry since 2020.",
s:[["Behavior","Chickens are social and form a ‘pecking order’, a hierarchy first described by Norwegian scientist Thorleif Schjelderup-Ebbe in 1921. They communicate with about 24 different calls, including alarms for predators from the air and the ground. Hens make special calls to their chicks and cluck to them while still in the egg."],["Eggs","A hen’s egg forms over about 25 hours. Hens lay eggs whether or not a rooster is present, but only fertilized eggs can hatch. Eggshell color depends on the breed; it does not affect taste or nutrition."],["Chickens and dinosaurs","Birds evolved from dinosaurs, and in 2007 scientists found that proteins from a Tyrannosaurus rex bone were similar to those of chickens. Chickens are not descended from T. rex, but they share a distant ancestor."],["Welfare and farming","Many countries have banned or are phasing out small ‘battery cages’ for hens; the European Union banned them in 2012. Fast-growing broiler breeds can suffer leg and heart problems. Campaigns push for cage-free eggs and slower-growing breeds."]],
k:[["Wild ancestor","Red junglefowl"],["Domesticated","About 3,500 years ago (2022 study)"],["Number alive","More than 25 billion"],["Eggs per hen","Up to about 300 a year"],["Social structure","Pecking order"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BC","Chickens domesticated in Southeast Asia"],["1921","Pecking order described"],["1948","‘Chicken of Tomorrow’ contest begins industrial breeding in the US"],["2007","T. rex proteins compared with chickens"],["2012","EU bans battery cages"],["2022","Study dates chicken domestication"]],
s2:[["Chickens in culture","The rooster is a symbol of France and one of the 12 animals of the Chinese zodiac. Ancient Romans consulted sacred chickens before battles, judging omens by how eagerly they ate. Cockfighting, still practiced in some countries, is banned in many others."],["Backyard chickens","Keeping a few hens in gardens has become popular in many cities. Owners collect fresh eggs and use chickens to eat pests and kitchen scraps, though they must protect them from foxes and follow local rules."]],
sp:["Bird flu","Highly pathogenic avian influenza H5N1 spread worldwide after 2020, killing wild birds and poultry. In 2024, it was found in dairy cattle in the United States, raising concern about spread to mammals and people."],
g:[["Domesticated","Bred and kept by people"],["Broiler","A chicken raised for meat"],["Pecking order","A social ranking among animals"],["Avian influenza","A virus that infects birds"]],
f:["Chickens are the world’s most numerous bird.","Chickens descend from the red junglefowl.","Eggshell color does not affect taste."],
fun:"There are about three chickens for every person on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"cattle",t:"Cattle",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐄",w:"Cattle",r:["domestication","agri","bison","climchange","indiacountry"],
p:[["Ancient partners","People domesticated cattle from wild aurochs about 10,500 years ago."],["Milk, meat and muscle","Cattle provide milk, beef, leather and pulling power."],["Climate impact","Cattle produce methane, a powerful greenhouse gas."]],
l:"Cattle are large domesticated hoofed mammals raised for meat (beef and veal), milk, leather and as draft animals for pulling plows and carts. There are about 1.5 billion cattle in the world. They descend from the aurochs, a huge wild ox that roamed Europe, Asia and North Africa; people domesticated aurochs about 10,500 years ago in the Near East, producing humpless taurine cattle, and separately in South Asia, producing humped zebu cattle. The last aurochs died in Poland in 1627. Cattle are ruminants with four-chambered stomachs, which let microbes break down grass and other tough plants; they bring food back up to chew as cud. This digestion produces methane, a powerful greenhouse gas, making cattle a major source of agricultural emissions. Brazil, India, the United States and China have the largest cattle herds. In Hinduism, cows are sacred, and their slaughter is banned in most Indian states. Cattle ranching is a leading driver of deforestation in the Amazon.",
s:[["Digestion","A cow’s stomach has four compartments: the rumen, reticulum, omasum and abomasum. Microbes in the rumen ferment plant material, and the cow regurgitates and rechews it. A dairy cow may spend about eight hours a day chewing cud."],["Breeds","There are more than 1,000 cattle breeds, including Holstein-Friesians, the black-and-white dairy cows that produce the most milk; beef breeds such as Angus and Hereford; and zebu breeds such as Brahman, adapted to heat and insects. Japan’s Wagyu cattle produce heavily marbled beef."],["Cattle and climate","Livestock produce about 12 percent of human-caused greenhouse gas emissions, according to the Food and Agriculture Organization, with cattle the largest share. Methane from cows mostly comes from burping. Feed additives such as seaweed and the chemical 3-NOP can reduce methane emissions, and some people eat less beef for environmental reasons."],["Sacred cows","In India, cows are revered as symbols of life and motherhood, and many Hindus do not eat beef. In parts of East Africa, cattle are central to wealth, marriage and identity for peoples such as the Maasai and Dinka."]],
k:[["Wild ancestor","Aurochs (extinct 1627)"],["Domesticated","About 10,500 years ago"],["World population","About 1.5 billion"],["Stomach","Four chambers (ruminant)"],["Main greenhouse gas","Methane"]],
d:[["c. 8500 BC","Aurochs domesticated in the Near East"],["c. 6000 BC","Zebu cattle domesticated in South Asia"],["1627","The last aurochs dies in Poland"],["1860s–1880s","Great cattle drives in the American West"],["2024","Bird flu found in US dairy cattle"]],
s2:[["Cattle drives","After the American Civil War, cowboys drove millions of Texas longhorn cattle north along trails such as the Chisholm Trail to railroads in Kansas, giving rise to the image of the cowboy. Railways and barbed wire ended the great drives by the late 1880s."],["Bringing back the aurochs","Breeders in Europe are trying to recreate aurochs-like cattle by crossing old breeds, to graze wild landscapes as the aurochs once did. In the 1930s, German brothers bred Heck cattle with the same aim."]],
sp:["Cows have best friends","Studies suggest that cows form close bonds with particular herd members, and their heart rates and stress levels fall when they are with a preferred companion."],
g:[["Ruminant","An animal that chews cud and has a multi-chambered stomach"],["Aurochs","The extinct wild ancestor of cattle"],["Zebu","Humped cattle from South Asia"],["Methane","A powerful greenhouse gas"]],
f:["Cattle descend from the extinct aurochs.","A cow’s stomach has four chambers.","Most cow methane comes from burping."],
fun:"Cows can recognize more than 50 individual members of their herd."});

ADDART({id:"mangoes",t:"Mangoes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥭",w:"Mango",r:["fruits","indiacountry","trade","coconut","culturalrev"],
p:[["King of fruits","Mangoes are sweet tropical fruits loved across Asia and beyond."],["From South Asia","Mangoes were domesticated in India thousands of years ago."],["Cashew cousins","Mangoes belong to the same family as cashews and poison ivy."]],
l:"The mango is the fruit of Mangifera indica, a large evergreen tree of the cashew family native to South Asia, where it has been cultivated for at least 4,000 years. Mango trees can grow 30 meters tall and live for centuries. The fruit is a drupe, with a single large flat seed surrounded by sweet, juicy flesh, and comes in hundreds of varieties that differ in color, size, flavor and texture, such as Alphonso and Kesar from India, Carabao from the Philippines, Ataulfo from Mexico and Kent and Tommy Atkins grown widely for export. India grows more mangoes than any other country, about 40 percent of the world’s supply, followed by Indonesia, China and Pakistan. Mangoes are eaten fresh, dried, in juices, chutneys and desserts, and green mangoes are used in pickles and salads. The mango is the national fruit of India, Pakistan and the Philippines. Mango skin and sap contain urushiol, the same irritant as in poison ivy, which can cause rashes in sensitive people.",
s:[["Spread around the world","Buddhist monks are said to have carried mangoes across Asia, and Persian and Arab traders brought them to East Africa. Portuguese traders introduced mangoes to Brazil and West Africa in the 16th century, and they spread to the Caribbean and Mexico. Today mangoes grow throughout the tropics."],["Varieties","There are more than 1,000 named varieties. Indian varieties are often sweet and fiberless, while many export varieties were bred in Florida in the 20th century for color and shelf life. ‘Polyembryonic’ Southeast Asian mangoes produce several seedlings from one seed, identical to the parent."],["Mangoes in culture","Mango leaves decorate doorways at Hindu weddings and festivals, and the mango motif inspired the paisley pattern. The emperor Akbar planted an orchard of 100,000 mango trees in the 16th century. In India, summer mango season is celebrated with festivals."],["Mao’s mangoes","In 1968, during China’s Cultural Revolution, Mao Zedong gave a gift of mangoes from a Pakistani visitor to workers. The fruit, unfamiliar to most Chinese, became an object of worship; wax replicas were made and paraded in a short-lived ‘mango cult’."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Mangifera indica"],["Family","Cashew family"],["Origin","South Asia"],["Largest producer","India (about 40%)"],["National fruit of","India, Pakistan, the Philippines"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Mangoes cultivated in India"],["1500s","Portuguese spread mangoes to Africa and Brazil"],["1570s","Akbar plants a vast mango orchard"],["1968","Mango cult in China during the Cultural Revolution"],["2007","India resumes mango exports to the United States"]],
s2:[["Mango diplomacy","Pakistan and India have long sent boxes of mangoes to foreign leaders as goodwill gifts. In 2007, after a 17-year ban, the United States agreed to import Indian mangoes in exchange for allowing Indian imports of American motorcycles."],["Why mango sap stings","Mango trees contain urushiol, especially in the sap near the stem and the skin. Workers harvesting mangoes may get ‘mango dermatitis’, and people allergic to poison ivy may react to mango skin."]],
sp:["The Philippine mango","The Carabao mango, known for its sweetness, was once listed by Guinness World Records as the sweetest mango. Dried Philippine mangoes are a popular export."],
g:[["Drupe","A fruit with a single hard stone, like a peach"],["Urushiol","An oily irritant found in mango skin and poison ivy"],["Chutney","A spicy sauce made with fruit"],["Variety","A distinct type of a plant"]],
f:["India grows about 40 percent of the world’s mangoes.","Mangoes are related to cashews.","Mango skin can cause rashes."],
fun:"The paisley pattern, popular in clothing, is thought to be based on the shape of a mango or a teardrop."});

ADDART({id:"strawberries",t:"Strawberries",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍓",w:"Strawberry",r:["fruits","roses","apples","francecountry","chilecountry"],
p:[["Not a true berry","Botanically, a strawberry is not a berry."],["Seeds on the outside","Each ‘seed’ on a strawberry is actually a tiny fruit."],["A French-made hybrid","The garden strawberry was created by accident in 18th-century France."]],
l:"The garden strawberry is a widely grown plant of the rose family, cultivated for its sweet, red, fragrant fruit. Botanically, a strawberry is not a berry but an ‘accessory fruit’: the red flesh grows from the base of the flower, and the true fruits are the tiny yellow ‘seeds’, called achenes, on the outside, about 200 on each strawberry. Wild strawberries have been eaten since ancient times, but they were small. The modern garden strawberry arose in France in the 1750s as an accidental hybrid between two American species: the large-fruited Chilean strawberry, brought from Chile by French officer Amédée-François Frézier in 1714, and the flavorful North American Virginia strawberry. Strawberries now grow worldwide; China is the largest producer, followed by the United States, where California grows most of the crop. Strawberries spread by runners, horizontal stems that root to form new plants. They are rich in vitamin C. Strawberry farming relies heavily on pesticides and labor-intensive picking.",
s:[["An accidental hybrid","Frézier’s Chilean plants were all female and produced little fruit in France until they were planted near Virginia strawberries, which pollinated them. In 1766, botanist Antoine Nicolas Duchesne showed that the garden strawberry was a hybrid of the two species."],["Growing strawberries","Strawberries are perennial plants that grow low to the ground. Farmers often grow them on raised beds covered with plastic, and increasingly in greenhouses and vertical farms. ‘Day-neutral’ varieties fruit throughout the season, while others fruit once in early summer."],["Strawberries in culture","In Britain, strawberries and cream are a tradition at the Wimbledon tennis championships, where about 38 metric tons of strawberries are eaten each year. Strawberries have symbolized love and purity in European art."],["Wild strawberries","Wild woodland strawberries, or fraises des bois, are tiny but intensely flavored. Unlike garden strawberries, they are native to Europe and were gathered by the ancient Romans."]],
k:[["Family","Rose family"],["Fruit type","Accessory fruit"],["Achenes per fruit","About 200"],["Hybrid of","Chilean and Virginia strawberries"],["Largest producer","China"]],
d:[["1714","Frézier brings Chilean strawberries to France"],["1750s","Garden strawberry arises as a hybrid in France"],["1766","Duchesne explains the hybrid’s origin"],["1877","First Wimbledon championships, later famous for strawberries and cream"],["2010s","Vertical and greenhouse strawberry farming expands"]],
s2:[["The name","The origin of the word ‘strawberry’ is uncertain. It may come from the runners that ‘strew’ across the ground, or from the straw farmers place under the plants to keep fruit clean."],["The strawberry moon","The full moon in June is sometimes called the strawberry moon, a name used by some Native American peoples because it came at strawberry harvest time."]],
sp:["Plougastel","The town of Plougastel in Brittany, France, near where the first garden strawberries were grown, became famous for strawberry farming and has a strawberry museum."],
g:[["Accessory fruit","A fruit whose flesh comes from parts other than the ovary"],["Achene","A small dry fruit containing one seed"],["Runner","A horizontal stem that roots to form a new plant"],["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species"]],
f:["Strawberries are not true berries.","A strawberry has about 200 seeds on the outside.","The garden strawberry is a French-made hybrid of two American species."],
fun:"Strawberries are the only fruit that wears its seeds on the outside."});

ADDART({id:"avocados",t:"Avocados",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥑",w:"Avocado",r:["fruits","mexicocountry","drugtrade","waterscarcity","droughts"],
p:[["A fatty fruit","Avocados are fruits rich in healthy fats."],["From Mexico","Avocados were domesticated in Mexico and Central America."],["Green gold","Rising demand has brought wealth but also water and crime problems."]],
l:"The avocado is the fruit of Persea americana, a tree of the laurel family native to south-central Mexico and Central America, where people have eaten avocados for about 10,000 years and cultivated them for thousands. Avocados are unusual fruits: instead of sugar, their creamy flesh is rich in oils, mostly healthy monounsaturated fats, along with fiber and potassium. The fruit ripens only after it is picked. The Hass variety, with dark, bumpy skin, makes up about 80 percent of avocados sold worldwide; all Hass trees descend from a single tree grown by California postal worker Rudolph Hass, who patented it in 1935. Mexico is by far the largest producer, especially the state of Michoacán, followed by Colombia, Peru and the Dominican Republic. Avocado consumption has soared since the 2000s with the popularity of guacamole and avocado toast. Avocado farming needs a lot of water and has been linked to deforestation, and in Michoacán criminal cartels have extorted avocado growers.",
s:[["Ghost of the megafauna","Avocado seeds are large, and scientists think the fruit evolved to be eaten by giant animals such as ground sloths and mastodons, which swallowed them whole and spread the seeds. After these animals died out, humans became the main spreaders of avocados."],["The Hass avocado","Rudolph Hass bought a seedling in the 1920s and noticed its rich fruit. He patented it in 1935, but earned little from it. The mother tree in La Habra Heights, California, died of root rot in 2002."],["Ripening","Avocados do not ripen on the tree; they can hang for months and soften only after picking. Ripening is triggered by ethylene gas, so placing avocados in a paper bag with a banana speeds it up. Cut avocados brown when exposed to oxygen."],["Problems","An avocado may take about 300–600 liters of water to grow, depending on the region, and plantations in Chile and Mexico have strained water supplies. In Michoacán, cartels have demanded protection money from farmers. In 2022, the United States briefly suspended Mexican avocado imports after an inspector was threatened."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Persea americana"],["Family","Laurel family"],["Origin","Mexico and Central America"],["Main variety","Hass (about 80%)"],["Largest producer","Mexico"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Avocados eaten in Mexico"],["1519","Spanish conquerors encounter avocados"],["1935","Rudolph Hass patents the Hass avocado"],["1997","US lifts its ban on Mexican avocados"],["2002","The Hass mother tree dies"],["2022","US briefly halts Mexican avocado imports"]],
s2:[["The name","The word avocado comes from the Nahuatl word āhuacatl. Spanish speakers turned it into aguacate, and English speakers into ‘avocado’ and the old name ‘alligator pear’, after its bumpy skin."],["Avocados and pets","Avocado leaves, skin and seeds contain persin, a substance harmful to birds, horses and some other animals. Dogs and cats are less sensitive, but the large seed can block their intestines."]],
sp:["Guacamole","Guacamole, mashed avocado with lime, onion, chili and cilantro, was made by the Aztecs, whose name for it, ahuacamolli, meant ‘avocado sauce’."],
g:[["Monounsaturated fat","A kind of fat considered healthy"],["Ethylene","A gas that makes fruits ripen"],["Megafauna","Very large animals"],["Extortion","Getting money by threats"]],
f:["Avocados ripen only after picking.","All Hass avocados come from one tree.","Avocados are rich in fats rather than sugar."],
fun:"Botanically, an avocado is a large berry with a single seed."});

ADDART({id:"pumpkins",t:"Pumpkins and Squash",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🎃",w:"Cucurbita",r:["fruits","mexicocountry","festivals","agri","tomatoes"],
p:[["Ancient American crops","Squash was among the first plants domesticated in the Americas."],["The Three Sisters","Squash was grown with corn and beans by Indigenous farmers."],["Giant pumpkins","The record pumpkin, grown in 2026, weighed about 1,432 kilograms."]],
l:"Pumpkins and squash are the fruits of plants of the genus Cucurbita, in the gourd family, which also includes cucumbers and melons. They include pumpkins, zucchini (courgettes), butternut squash, acorn squash and spaghetti squash. Squash was domesticated in Mexico about 10,000 years ago, earlier than corn, making it one of the first crops in the Americas. Indigenous farmers across North America grew squash together with corn and beans in a system called the Three Sisters: corn provides a pole for beans to climb, beans add nitrogen to the soil, and squash leaves shade the ground to keep it moist and block weeds. Squash spread to Europe after 1492. Today China and India are the largest producers. Pumpkins are carved into jack-o’-lanterns at Halloween, a custom that began in Ireland and Britain with turnips. Competitive growers raise giant pumpkins; in October 2026, a pumpkin grown in Rhode Island set a world record of about 1,432 kilograms (3,156 pounds).",
s:[["Summer and winter squash","Summer squash, such as zucchini, is picked young when the skin is soft. Winter squash, such as pumpkins and butternut squash, is harvested when mature with a hard rind and can be stored for months. Squash flowers and seeds are also eaten."],["The Three Sisters","The Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) and many other Native American peoples planted corn, beans and squash together in mounds. The combination provides a balanced diet: carbohydrates from corn, protein from beans and vitamins from squash. Many gardeners still use the method today."],["Jack-o’-lanterns","In Ireland and Scotland, people carved turnips and potatoes into lanterns for the festival of Samhain. Irish immigrants in North America adopted pumpkins, which were larger and easier to carve. The name comes from a folk tale about ‘Stingy Jack’, who wandered with a lantern after tricking the Devil."],["Giant pumpkins","Giant pumpkin growers use the variety Atlantic Giant and feed plants carefully, and a big pumpkin can gain more than 20 kilograms a day at its peak. The world record rose from about 480 kilograms in 1996 to over 1,400 kilograms by 2026. Giant pumpkins are sometimes hollowed out and paddled as boats in races."]],
k:[["Genus","Cucurbita"],["Family","Gourd family"],["Domesticated","About 10,000 years ago in Mexico"],["Growing system","Three Sisters (corn, beans, squash)"],["Record pumpkin","About 1,432 kg (2026)"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Squash domesticated in Mexico"],["c. 1000","Three Sisters farming widespread in North America"],["1490s","Squash reaches Europe"],["1800s","Pumpkin jack-o’-lanterns spread in North America"],["2026","World-record pumpkin of about 1,432 kg"]],
s2:[["Pumpkin pie","Pumpkin pie became a traditional dessert at Thanksgiving in the United States in the 19th century, after writer Sarah Josepha Hale campaigned for the holiday. Most canned pumpkin is actually made from a type of squash that looks less like a classic pumpkin."],["Is it a fruit?","Botanically, pumpkins and squash are fruits, specifically a type of berry called a pepo, because they develop from flowers and contain seeds. In cooking they are treated as vegetables."]],
sp:["Pumpkin regattas","In several towns in North America and Europe, people hollow out giant pumpkins weighing hundreds of kilograms, climb in and paddle them across lakes in pumpkin regattas."],
g:[["Gourd family","A plant family including pumpkins, cucumbers and melons"],["Three Sisters","Corn, beans and squash grown together"],["Pepo","A berry with a hard rind, like a pumpkin"],["Jack-o’-lantern","A carved lantern, usually a pumpkin"]],
f:["Squash was domesticated before corn.","Jack-o’-lanterns were first carved from turnips.","The record pumpkin weighed about 1,432 kg."],
fun:"Giant pumpkins grow so quickly near their peak that they can gain more than 20 kilograms in a single day."});

ADDART({id:"brassicas",t:"Cabbage, Broccoli and Kale",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥦",w:"Brassica oleracea",r:["plantbased","vitamins","domestication","evolution","agri"],
p:[["One plant, many vegetables","Cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts are all one species."],["Bred by farmers","Farmers created them by selecting different parts of a wild cabbage."],["Bitter taste","Some people taste these vegetables as more bitter because of their genes."]],
l:"Cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts, kohlrabi and collard greens look very different, but they are all varieties of a single species, Brassica oleracea, descended from a wild cabbage that grows on coastal cliffs of Europe. Over thousands of years, farmers selected plants with particular features: large leaves produced kale and collards, a tight bud at the top produced cabbage, swollen side buds produced Brussels sprouts, enlarged flower clusters produced broccoli and cauliflower, and a thickened stem produced kohlrabi. They belong to the mustard family, which also includes turnips, radishes, mustard, bok choy, rapeseed (canola) and wasabi. These vegetables contain sulfur compounds called glucosinolates, which give them their sharp or bitter flavor and have been studied for possible health benefits. People with certain versions of the gene TAS2R38 taste some of these compounds as much more bitter, which may explain why some children and adults dislike Brussels sprouts.",
s:[["Artificial selection","The varieties of Brassica oleracea are a classic example of artificial selection, which Charles Darwin used to explain natural selection. Cabbage was grown by the ancient Greeks and Romans; broccoli was developed in Italy, cauliflower in the Mediterranean and Brussels sprouts probably near Brussels in the 1600s."],["Nutrition","These vegetables are rich in vitamins C and K, folate and fiber. Kale became a ‘superfood’ trend in the 2010s. Boiling for too long releases sulfur compounds that cause a strong smell, so steaming or roasting keeps flavor and nutrients."],["The mustard family","Relatives in the mustard family include turnips, radishes, watercress, horseradish and wasabi, whose heat comes from similar sulfur compounds. Rapeseed oil was bred in Canada in the 1970s into canola, low in bitter compounds."],["Fermentation","Cabbage is fermented into sauerkraut in Germany and kimchi in Korea, where napa cabbage, another brassica, is the main ingredient. Fermentation preserved vegetables for winter, and sailors ate sauerkraut to help prevent scurvy."]],
k:[["Species","Brassica oleracea"],["Ancestor","Wild cabbage of European coasts"],["Family","Mustard family"],["Flavor compounds","Glucosinolates"],["Bitter-taste gene","TAS2R38"]],
d:[["c. 600 BC","Cabbage grown in ancient Greece"],["1500s","Broccoli developed in Italy"],["1600s","Brussels sprouts grown near Brussels"],["1970s","Canada breeds canola from rapeseed"],["2010s","Kale becomes a ‘superfood’ trend"]],
s2:[["Sprouts are getting sweeter","Many people today find Brussels sprouts less bitter than in the past. In the 1990s, Dutch breeders identified compounds causing bitterness and crossed old varieties to produce milder sprouts, helping their popularity grow."],["Romanesco","Romanesco broccoli has a striking spiral pattern in which each bud is made of smaller buds, a natural example of a fractal. The number of spirals often follows the Fibonacci sequence."]],
sp:["Wild cabbage","Wild cabbage still grows on chalk and limestone cliffs in Britain, France and other parts of Europe. It is a tough plant with thick, waxy leaves that store water and resist salt spray."],
g:[["Artificial selection","Breeding by choosing plants with desired traits"],["Glucosinolate","A sulfur compound in mustard-family plants"],["Fermentation","Changing food using microbes"],["Fractal","A pattern that repeats at smaller scales"]],
f:["Broccoli and cabbage are the same species.","Brussels sprouts have become less bitter through breeding.","Kimchi is made mainly from napa cabbage."],
fun:"Broccoli, cauliflower and kohlrabi were all bred from the same wild cabbage plant, which you can still find growing on European sea cliffs."});

ADDART({id:"oaks",t:"Oak Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌳",w:"Oak",r:["trees","squirrels","deforestation","ships","grapes"],
p:[["Mighty trees","Oaks are long-lived trees and shrubs that produce acorns."],["Homes for wildlife","A single oak can support thousands of other species."],["Wood for ships and wine","Oak wood built navies and ages wine and whisky."]],
l:"Oaks are trees and shrubs of the genus Quercus, in the beech family, with about 500 species found across the Northern Hemisphere, from Europe and Asia to North and Central America. They range from small shrubs to giant trees more than 40 meters tall, and many live for hundreds of years; some exceed 1,000 years. Oaks produce acorns, nuts held in cup-shaped bases, which feed squirrels, jays, deer, wild boars and many other animals. In some years, called mast years, oaks over a large area produce huge crops of acorns at once, overwhelming seed-eaters so some acorns survive. Oaks support remarkable biodiversity: in Britain, a 2019 study found that more than 2,300 species of insects, fungi, birds, lichens and other organisms use native oaks. Oak wood is hard, strong and resistant to decay and was used to build houses, cathedrals and sailing warships; oak barrels give flavor to wine and whisky. Oaks are symbols of strength in many cultures and national trees of countries including the United States, England and Germany.",
s:[["Acorns","Acorns contain bitter tannins, which animals and people must remove before eating them in large amounts. Native Americans in California leached acorns in water to make flour, a major food source for thousands of years. Jays bury thousands of acorns each autumn, planting new oak forests."],["Oak and ships","A large sailing warship such as HMS Victory, launched in 1765, needed about 6,000 trees, mostly oak. Britain and France planted oak forests to supply future navies, though by the time they matured, ships were built of iron."],["Wine and whisky barrels","Oak barrels let small amounts of oxygen in and add flavors such as vanilla and spice to wine, whisky and brandy. French and American oaks give different flavors, and coopers char the insides of whisky barrels."],["Threats","Diseases such as sudden oak death, caused by a fungus-like organism discovered in California in the 1990s, have killed millions of oaks. Acute oak decline, oak wilt and pests such as the oak processionary moth also threaten trees, and climate change may shift where oaks can grow."]],
k:[["Genus","Quercus"],["Species","About 500"],["Fruit","Acorn"],["Lifespan","Often hundreds of years"],["Species supported (UK)","More than 2,300"]],
d:[["c. 1000","The Major Oak in Sherwood Forest begins to grow (estimated)"],["1765","HMS Victory launched, built from about 6,000 trees"],["1995","Sudden oak death identified in California"],["2004","The oak becomes the national tree of the United States"],["2019","Study counts species that depend on British oaks"]],
s2:[["Famous oaks","The Major Oak in Sherwood Forest, England, linked in legend to Robin Hood, is about 800–1,000 years old. The Royal Oak, in which the future King Charles II hid after a battle in 1651, became a national symbol, and many pubs are named after it."],["Iron gall ink","For more than 1,000 years, writers used ink made from oak galls, growths caused by wasps, mixed with iron salts. Iron gall ink was used for many famous documents, including the United States Constitution and Leonardo da Vinci’s notebooks."]],
sp:["Mast years","Scientists think oaks synchronize mast years using weather cues, such as warm, dry springs. In mast years, mice and deer populations boom the following year, which can increase ticks and Lyme disease."],
g:[["Acorn","The nut of an oak tree"],["Mast year","A year with a huge seed crop"],["Tannin","A bitter substance in oak bark and acorns"],["Cooper","A person who makes barrels"]],
f:["There are about 500 species of oak.","British oaks support more than 2,300 species.","HMS Victory used about 6,000 trees."],
fun:"An oak may not produce acorns until it is about 20 to 50 years old, and its biggest crops come when it is about 80 to 120 years old."});

ADDART({id:"legumes",t:"Beans and Legumes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🫘",w:"Legume",r:["plantbased","agri","mendel","nutri","soil"],
p:[["Seeds in pods","Legumes include beans, peas, lentils, chickpeas and peanuts."],["Natural fertilizer","Legumes work with bacteria to add nitrogen to the soil."],["Protein-rich","They are an important source of protein around the world."]],
l:"Legumes are plants of the pea family, Fabaceae, one of the largest plant families, with about 20,000 species including trees, shrubs and herbs. Their fruits are pods containing seeds. The dried edible seeds, called pulses, include beans, lentils, chickpeas and dry peas, while soybeans and peanuts are grown mainly for oil and protein. Legumes were among the first crops domesticated: lentils, peas and chickpeas in the Fertile Crescent about 10,000 years ago, and beans in the Americas. A special feature of legumes is their partnership with soil bacteria called rhizobia, which live in nodules on their roots and convert nitrogen from the air into a form plants can use. This enriches the soil, so farmers rotate legumes with other crops. Legumes are rich in protein, fiber and minerals and are central to diets in South Asia, Latin America, Africa and the Middle East. Gregor Mendel used pea plants to discover the laws of heredity. Brazil and the United States are the largest soybean producers.",
s:[["Nitrogen fixation","Rhizobia bacteria enter legume roots and form nodules, pinkish lumps containing a protein like hemoglobin. The plant supplies sugars, and the bacteria supply nitrogen compounds. A legume crop can add tens of kilograms of nitrogen per hectare, reducing the need for chemical fertilizer."],["Pulses around the world","Lentils and chickpeas are the basis of Indian dal and Middle Eastern hummus and falafel. Black beans and rice are staples in Latin America, and soybeans are made into tofu, soy sauce and miso in East Asia. The United Nations declared 2016 the International Year of Pulses."],["Soybeans","Soybeans were domesticated in China several thousand years ago and are now one of the world’s most important crops, mostly used for animal feed and vegetable oil. Expanding soybean farming in Brazil has contributed to deforestation in the Amazon and Cerrado."],["Mendel’s peas","Between 1856 and 1863, Gregor Mendel grew about 28,000 pea plants in his monastery garden, tracking traits such as flower color and seed shape. He discovered patterns of inheritance that founded the science of genetics."]],
k:[["Family","Fabaceae (pea family)"],["Species","About 20,000"],["Dried seeds called","Pulses"],["Root partners","Rhizobia bacteria"],["Largest soybean producer","Brazil"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Lentils, peas and chickpeas domesticated"],["c. 1000 BC","Soybeans widely farmed in China"],["1856–1863","Mendel’s pea experiments"],["1888","Nitrogen-fixing root bacteria discovered"],["2016","International Year of Pulses"]],
s2:[["Beans and wind","Beans contain sugars called oligosaccharides that humans cannot digest. Bacteria in the large intestine ferment them, producing gas. Soaking and cooking beans reduces the effect."],["Peanuts","Peanuts are legumes, not nuts. Their flowers grow above ground, but after pollination the stalks bend down and push the developing pods into the soil, where they mature. Peanut allergies are among the most common food allergies."]],
sp:["Favism","Some people, especially in Mediterranean, African and Asian populations, have an inherited condition called G6PD deficiency. Eating fava beans can cause a dangerous breakdown of their red blood cells, a reaction called favism."],
g:[["Pulse","A dried edible legume seed"],["Rhizobia","Bacteria that fix nitrogen in legume roots"],["Nitrogen fixation","Turning nitrogen gas into usable compounds"],["Crop rotation","Growing different crops in turn on the same land"]],
f:["Legumes add nitrogen to the soil.","Peanuts are legumes, not nuts.","Mendel discovered heredity using peas."],
fun:"Peanut pods ripen underground after the flower stalk bends down and plants them in the soil."});
